"Hello everyone, my name is Javas Maria, and my part of this project focuses on gasoline prices, fuel consumption, and the financial impact of transportation on residents in Cuba, New Mexico.
I wanted to explore how the cost of gasoline affects people's everyday lives and how it contributes to transportation challenges in this rural community."
Slide 13 – Gas Prices near Cuba, NM
"First, we looked at gasoline prices in Cuba compared to the rest of New Mexico.
The average gas price in Cuba is $4.45 per gallon, compared to the state average of $4.02. That's a difference of 43 cents per gallon.
Although this may not seem like a lot, these additional costs can add up over time, especially for residents who rely on personal vehicles for their daily transportation."
Slide 14 – Gasoline Consumption
"Next, we looked at how gasoline costs increase depending on the amount of fuel needed.
At Cuba's average gas price, every additional five gallons costs $22.25. Filling up a 20-gallon tank would cost around $89.
This is especially important in rural communities like Cuba, where residents may need to travel longer distances for work, school, healthcare, and other essential services."
Slide 15 – Gasoline Consumption Comparison
"We then compared annual gasoline consumption in Cuba to the statewide average.
In New Mexico, the average gasoline consumption is approximately 427.5 gallons per person per year.
For Cuba, we estimated approximately 527.6 gallons per person per year, accounting for longer rural travel distances.
That's about 110 additional gallons per year, which, combined with higher gas prices, creates an even greater financial burden for residents."
Slide 16 – Income in Cuba, NM
"Finally, we compared gasoline expenses to income.
The average annual per capita income in Cuba is about $24,855, which is significantly lower than both the state and national averages.
Based on our estimates, Cuba residents could spend around 9.45 percent of their income on gasoline alone, compared to 4.74 percent statewide and 3.08 percent nationwide.
That's almost one-tenth of their income going toward gasoline, leaving less money for food, housing, and other family needs.
From a peace engineering perspective, these findings highlight the importance of creating more affordable and accessible transportation solutions to reduce financial burdens and improve the quality of life for residents in Cuba."
